\import LambdaBox
-- Only `map`/`mkArray`/`Big`: importing Data.Array wholesale would shadow
-- String's `++` with the Array one, so array append is imported under the
-- alias `++A` (used in `appendStmts` and `compileClass` below).
\import Data.Array (map, mkArray, Big, ++ \as \infixr 5 ++A)
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
\import JavaAst
\import JavaPrint
\import Set
\import StringUtil

-- =============================================================================
-- lambda-box (LBTerm) -> Java generator.
--
-- It builds `JavaAst` values (`JavaAst.ard`), NOT source text: the target
-- language is defined by that AST, and spelling it out is the sole job of the
-- trusted printer (`JavaPrint.ard`). `compileProgramWith` composes the two, so
-- callers still get a `String`.
--
-- Termination approach: Arend's foetus/size-change checker rejects a separate
-- list-typed helper that calls compileExpr (it crosses the LBTerm <-> Array
-- LBTerm type boundary). So, following arend-lib's HRS.ard
-- (`\lam i => apply (arguments i)`), compileExpr recurses over Array children
-- INLINE via `xs i` / direct projections like `(brs i).2` (same type, indexed
-- sub-term / direct projection of the recursion argument) -- never through a
-- `\case`-bound variable, which loses the structural link the checker needs.
-- For the same reason `Data.Array.map` cannot be used HERE: it hides `args i`
-- behind a closure (verified: the checker rejects it), so the inline arrays are
-- built with `mkArray {..} {xs.len} (\lam i => .. (xs i))`.
-- The per-child results are then combined by array operations (`Data.Array`'s
-- `++`/`Big`/`map`) that do NOT call compileExpr, so they terminate on their
-- own; `map` is fine there and everywhere outside the recursion.
--
-- No fresh-name counter: a structural `path : String` is passed DOWN and
-- branched per child, so names are unique without left-to-right state
-- threading (which would force a sequential helper).
--
-- Design (unchanged): untyped -> Java `Object`; closures = anonymous inner
-- classes (no invokedynamic); constructors -> `Data{int tag; Object[] fields}`;
-- constants -> zero-arg static methods; fix -> mutable `Fn[]` knot-tying.
--
-- The fixed part of that encoding (`Fn`, `Data` + its `toString`, `BOX`) is NOT
-- generated: it lives in the hand-written `runtime/Rt.java` shipped alongside
-- the output, and generated code refers to it through `rt` below.
--
-- Everything target-specific (the runtime class name and the representation of
-- λ□ primitive ints) is collected in the `JavaTarget` record below, which the
-- generator takes as an instance parameter instead of threading a
-- representation argument through every definition.
-- =============================================================================

-- A Java compilation target: the runtime it talks to plus the representation it
-- picks for λ□ primitive ints. This is a configuration record (no classifying
-- field), so it is passed EXPLICITLY once at the top (`compileProgramWith`) and
-- inferred from there on, as a local instance, by everything it calls.
--
-- Adding a new representation is adding a new value of this record -- no new
-- constructor and no new `\elim` clause anywhere in the generator.
--
-- Representation of λ□ primitive ints in the generated Java (`intLit` /
-- `primOpSuffix`).
--
-- KNOWN MISMATCH (documented, not fixed). A λ□ `prim (primInt, _)` is a 63-bit
-- machine integer with cyclic (mod 2^63) arithmetic -- MetaRocq's
-- erasure/theories/EPrimitive.v has `primIntModel (i : PrimInt63.int)`,
-- lean-to-lambdabox has `PrimModel .primInt := BitVec 63`, and the Rocq refman
-- ("Primitive objects / Primitive integers") specifies the width plus the
-- unsigned/signed views `Uint63`/`Sint63`. No Java type matches that, and our
-- own AST does not either (LambdaBox.ard's `PrimModel primInt => Int` is
-- unbounded). So both targets below are approximations, and the caller picks:
--   * `targetLong`       -- java.lang.Long: Java's built-in 64-bit integer. The
--     DEFAULT (see `compileProgram`), because it is the closer approximation:
--     it is a fixed-width integer with wraparound, so only the width is wrong
--     (2^64 instead of 2^63) and the sign convention differs, whereas
--     `PrimInt63` reduction is unsigned/cyclic. It is also much faster.
--   * `targetBigInteger` -- java.math.BigInteger: unbounded, never overflows,
--     but boxed arithmetic and NO wraparound. Matches OUR AST's `Int`, not the
--     source's int63 nor the C/OCaml int63 runtimes, so it is further from the
--     source semantics than `targetLong`, not more conservative. Kept because
--     unbounded arithmetic is occasionally useful when debugging.
-- Both agree with the C backend (CertiRocq's prim_int63_*) and the OCaml one
-- (unboxed OCaml `int`, cf. ExtrOCamlInt63) only while values stay well below
-- 2^62, which holds for the current examples.
-- FUTURE WORK: a third `targetInt63` value whose literals and
-- `Rt.PRIM_*` ops normalize mod 2^63 (following `Uint63`), which is the only
-- one that could be proved to agree with λ□'s evaluation relation once that
-- exists. See LambdaBox.ard's `PrimModel` and RESEARCH_AND_PLAN.md.
--
-- Everything below is parameterized by this choice; there is no default hidden
-- inside the generator (see `compileProgramWith` / `compileProgram`).
\class JavaTarget
  -- Name of the runtime class (see runtime/Rt.java); `rt` below is the qualifier
  -- used in every emitted reference, so retargeting the runtime is one field.
  | runtimeClass : String
  -- A `prim (primInt, i)` literal in this target's representation.
  | intLit : Int -> JExpr
  -- Suffix of the runtime's primitive-op constants (`Rt.PRIM_ADD_INT` vs
  -- `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`); the families are otherwise identical in ABI.
  | primOpSuffix : String

-- Qualifier for every runtime reference (`Rt.`), used to build qualified names
-- for `jCallStatic`/`tClass`; `rtRef` is the same thing as an expression.
\func rt {t : JavaTarget} : String => runtimeClass ++ "."

\func rtRef {t : JavaTarget} : JExpr => jStatic runtimeClass

\func rtFn {t : JavaTarget} : JType => tClass (rt ++ "Fn")

\func rtData {t : JavaTarget} : JType => tClass (rt ++ "Data")

\func targetLong : JavaTarget \cowith
  | runtimeClass => "Rt"
  | intLit i => jCallStatic "Long.valueOf" (jLongLit i :: nil)
  | primOpSuffix => "LONG"

\func targetBigInteger : JavaTarget \cowith
  | runtimeClass => "Rt"
  | intLit i => jNew (tClass "java.math.BigInteger") (jStringLit (intToStr i) :: nil)
  | primOpSuffix => "INT"

-- Realization of λ□ axioms (constants without a body) in the Java runtime.
-- Peregrine does this per run via `--attributes` (see ExampleMatMul.ard's
-- matMulAttrsC/OCaml, remapping onto CertiRocq's prim_int63_*); the Java
-- backend instead has this fixed table of axiom name -> runtime expression,
-- since the runtime it targets (`runtime/Rt.java`) is ours. An axiom listed
-- here compiles to the runtime constant, everything else to a throwing stub.
-- Keys are fully mangled kernames: the Lean frontend places machine-Nat
-- primitives under `Nat`, while the hand-written examples use root names.
-- Keeping the module path avoids treating an unrelated `Foo.add` as Nat.add.
\func javaAxioms {t : JavaTarget} : Array (\Sigma String JExpr) =>
  \let | op (nm : String) => jField rtRef ("PRIM_" ++ nm ++ "_" ++ primOpSuffix)
       -- An operation that neither takes nor returns a λ□ primitive int is the
       -- same in both representations, so it has ONE runtime constant.
       | fixed (nm : String) => jField rtRef nm
  \in ("c___prim_add_int", op "ADD") ::
      ("c___prim_mul_int", op "MUL") ::
      ("c___prim_sub_int", op "SUB") ::
      ("c___prim_eqb_int", op "EQB") ::
      ("c__Nat__add", op "ADD") ::
      ("c__Nat__mul", op "MUL") ::
      -- NOT `op "SUB"`: Lean's Nat subtraction truncates at zero, while
      -- `prim_sub_int` is Peregrine's wrapping int63 subtraction.
      ("c__Nat__sub", jField rtRef ("NAT_SUB_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Nat__beq", op "EQB") ::
      -- The rest of the machine-Nat operations lean-to-lambdabox leaves as
      -- axioms. `div`/`mod` follow Lean's total conventions and
      -- `decEq`/`decLe`/`decLt` return `Decidable`, not `Bool` (see Rt.java).
      ("c__Nat__div", op "DIV") ::
      ("c__Nat__mod", op "MOD") ::
      ("c__Nat__pow", op "POW") ::
      ("c__Nat__ble", op "BLE") ::
      ("c__Nat__blt", op "BLT") ::
      ("c__Nat__decEq", op "DEC_EQ") ::
      ("c__Nat__decLe", op "DEC_LE") ::
      ("c__Nat__decLt", op "DEC_LT") ::
      -- Lean's machine-`Int` operations. `Int` erases to the same λ□ primitive
      -- int as `Nat`, so `ofNat` is the identity in both representations.
      ("c__Int__ofNat", fixed "INT_OF_NAT") ::
      ("c__Int__negSucc", jField rtRef ("INT_NEG_SUCC_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__neg", jField rtRef ("INT_NEG_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__add", jField rtRef ("INT_ADD_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__mul", jField rtRef ("INT_MUL_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__ediv", jField rtRef ("INT_EDIV_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__emod", jField rtRef ("INT_EMOD_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__decEq", jField rtRef ("INT_DEC_EQ_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      -- Lean's `Array` operations (`@[extern]`, so erasure keeps no body). Only
      -- `size` returns a Nat, hence only it depends on the representation. The
      -- Lean frontend writes `!` in a name as `_u33`.
      ("c__Array__mk", fixed "ARRAY_MK") ::
      ("c__Array__emptyWithCapacity", fixed "ARRAY_EMPTY_WITH_CAPACITY") ::
      ("c__Array__push", fixed "ARRAY_PUSH") ::
      ("c__Array__getInternal", fixed "ARRAY_GET_INTERNAL") ::
      ("c__Array__get_u33Internal", fixed "ARRAY_GET_BANG_INTERNAL") ::
      ("c__Array__set_u33", fixed "ARRAY_SET_BANG") ::
      ("c__Array__swap", fixed "ARRAY_SWAP") ::
      ("c__Array__size", jField rtRef ("ARRAY_SIZE_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      -- Rewriting along an equality is the identity at runtime.
      ("c__Eq__rec", fixed "EQ_REC") ::
      ("c__Eq__ndrec", fixed "EQ_REC") ::
      nil

-- Lookup by fully mangled kername, including its module path.
\func lookupAxiom (name : String) (tbl : Array (\Sigma String JExpr)) : Maybe JExpr \elim tbl
  | nil => nothing
  | e :: tbl => \case e.1 == name \with {
    | true => just e.2
    | false => lookupAxiom name tbl
  }

-- Compilation result: (statements to run first, value expression).
\func Out : \Type => \Sigma (Array JStmt) JExpr

-- The environment maps a de Bruijn index to the EXPRESSION that denotes it:
-- usually a variable (`jVar "p3"`), but a `fix` slot is an array element
-- (`x3[0]`), which is why this is an `Array JExpr` and not an `Array JIdent`.
-- Head = index 0.
-- An index larger than the number of enclosing binders is ill-formed input (a
-- closed λ□ program has none), so instead of a silent `null` we emit a runtime
-- call that fails immediately and reports the index and the node.  `n0` is the
-- original index, kept for the message while `n` counts down.
\func nthVar {t : JavaTarget} (n : Nat) (env : Array JExpr) (n0 : Nat) (path : String) : JExpr \elim n, env
  | _, nil => jCallStatic (rt ++ "unbound") (jIntLit n0 :: jStringLit path :: nil)
  | 0, x :: _ => x
  | suc n, _ :: env => nthVar n env n0 path

-- Array append and flattening of per-child results. Both come from Data.Array;
-- neither calls compileExpr, so the recursion above stays in its own SCC.
-- `appendStmts` is the main place the aliased array append `++A` is used (see
-- the import).
\func appendStmts (xs ys : Array JStmt) : Array JStmt => xs ++A ys

\func concatStmtBlocks (bs : Array (Array JStmt)) : Array JStmt => Big appendStmts nil bs

-- The statements of a sequence of child results, in order.
\func concatStmts (rs : Array Out) : Array JStmt => concatStmtBlocks (map (\lam r => r.1) rs)

-- Kername -> Java method name. Every source component is encoded at this one
-- boundary; the fixed separators and leading `c_` are legal by construction.
\func joinDirs (ds : Array String) : String \elim ds
  | nil => ""
  | d :: nil => javaIdentPart d
  | d :: ds => javaIdentPart d ++ "_" ++ joinDirs ds

\func mangleModPath (mp : ModPath) : String \elim mp
  | mpFile dp => joinDirs dp
  | mpBound dp id n => joinDirs dp ++ "_" ++ javaIdentPart id ++ "_" ++ natToStr n
  | mpDot mp id => mangleModPath mp ++ "_" ++ javaIdentPart id

\func mangleKername (k : Kername) : String =>
  "c_" ++ mangleModPath k.kerModPath ++ "__" ++ javaIdentPart k.kerName

-- Bind a branch's fields to fresh locals, starting at field index `fieldIx`
-- (= npars, to skip inductive parameters). Returns (bind-statements, extended env).
-- Recurses on the binder array only; never calls compileExpr.
-- Pushing left-to-right makes the LAST binder de Bruijn index 0 (matches iota_red).
\func bindFields (dv : JExpr) (fieldIx : Nat) (bs : Array BinderName) (env : Array JExpr) (path : String)
  : \Sigma (Array JStmt) (Array JExpr) \elim bs
  | nil => (nil, env)
  | _ :: bs =>
    \let | nm => "g" ++ natToStr fieldIx ++ path
         | st => jLocal true tObject nm (just (jIndex (jField dv "fields") (jIntLit fieldIx)))
         | r  => bindFields dv (suc fieldIx) bs (jVar nm :: env) path
    \in (st :: r.1, r.2)

-- Push the k fix slots arr[0..k-1] onto env; head = arr[k-1] = de Bruijn 0
-- (= the LAST mutually-defined function, matching λ□'s fix_subst). No compileExpr.
\func pushFix (arr : String) (j k : Nat) (env : Array JExpr) : Array JExpr \elim k
  | 0 => env
  | suc k => pushFix arr (suc j) k (jIndex (jVar arr) (jIntLit j) :: env)

-- Core compiler. `path` uniquely identifies this node; binder names and child
-- paths are derived from it, so no state threading is needed.
\func compileExpr {tgt : JavaTarget} (env : Array JExpr) (path : String) (t : LBTerm) : Out \elim t
  | box => (nil, jField rtRef "BOX")

  | bvar n => (nil, nthVar n env n path)

  -- A locally-nameless free variable has no Java counterpart at all (the
  -- serializer likewise emits `tVar_UNREACHABLE`), so compile it to a throwing
  -- runtime call naming the variable rather than to a silent `null`.
  | fvar v => (nil, jCallStatic (rt ++ "freeVar") (jStringLit v.varName :: jStringLit path :: nil))

  | prim (primInt, i) => (nil, intLit i)

  | const k => (nil, jCallStatic (mangleKername k) nil)

  | app f a =>
    \let | of => compileExpr env ("F" ++ path) f
         | oa => compileExpr env ("A" ++ path) a
    \in (appendStmts of.1 oa.1, jInvoke (jCast rtFn of.2) "apply" (oa.2 :: nil))

  | lambda _ body =>
    \let | pv => "p" ++ path
         | ob => compileExpr (jVar pv :: env) ("L" ++ path) body
    \in (nil, jAnonFn rtFn pv (appendStmts ob.1 (jReturn ob.2 :: nil)))

  | letIn _ value body =>
    \let | ov => compileExpr env ("V" ++ path) value
         | lv => "l" ++ path
         | ob => compileExpr (jVar lv :: env) ("B" ++ path) body
    \in (appendStmts ov.1 (jLocal true tObject lv (just ov.2) :: ob.1), ob.2)

  -- construct: recurse inline over the Array of args.
  | construct _ cidx args =>
    \let | results => mkArray {Out} {args.len} (\lam i => compileExpr env ("c" ++ natToStr i ++ path) (args i))
    \in (concatStmts results,
         jNew rtData (jIntLit cidx ::
                      jNewArrayInit tObject (map (\lam r => r.2) results) ::
                      nil))

  -- case: switch on the scrutinee's tag; each branch binds fields[npars ..] then
  -- evaluates its body. We map inline over `brs i`, binding the body by pattern.
  -- The switch has no value, so the branches assign to a result local `r<path>`,
  -- which is this node's value expression.
  | case _ npars scrut brs =>
    \let | os => compileExpr env ("S" ++ path) scrut
         | dv => "d" ++ path
         | rv => "r" ++ path
         -- Recurse on the DIRECT projection `(brs i).2`, NOT a `\case`-bound
         -- variable: the checker tracks `(brs i).2 < t` (index into the sub-term
         -- `brs`, then project) but loses that through a `\case` pattern binding.
         | cases => mkArray {\Sigma Nat (Array JStmt)} {brs.len} (\lam i =>
             \let | bp  => "b" ++ natToStr i ++ path
                  | bnd => bindFields (jVar dv) npars (brs i).1 env bp
                  | ob  => compileExpr bnd.2 bp (brs i).2
             \in (i, appendStmts bnd.1 (appendStmts ob.1 (jAssign (jVar rv) ob.2 :: nil))))
    \in (appendStmts os.1
           (jLocal true rtData dv (just (jCast rtData os.2)) ::
            jLocal false tObject rv nothing ::
            jSwitch (jField (jVar dv) "tag") cases
              (jThrow (jNew (tClass "RuntimeException") (jStringLit "no matching branch" :: nil)) :: nil) ::
            nil),
         jVar rv)

  -- proj: the AST has no arithmetic node, so `npars + idx` is added here.
  | proj pinfo scrut =>
    \let | os => compileExpr env ("P" ++ path) scrut
    \in (os.1,
         jIndex (jField (jCast rtData os.2) "fields")
                (jIntLit (pinfo.projParamCount Nat.+ pinfo.projFieldIdx)))

  -- fix: mutable Fn[] + knot-tying; map inline over `defs j`, projecting the
  -- body directly (`(defs j).defBody`), same reasoning as `case` above.
  | fix defs idx =>
    \let | arr => "x" ++ path
         | k   => defs.len
         | env' => pushFix arr 0 k env
         | assigns => mkArray {Array JStmt} {k} (\lam j =>
             \let | ob => compileExpr env' ("y" ++ natToStr j ++ path) (defs j).defBody
             \in appendStmts ob.1 (jAssign (jIndex (jVar arr) (jIntLit j)) (jCast rtFn ob.2) :: nil)
         )
    \in (jLocal true (tArray rtFn) arr (just (jNewArraySized rtFn (jIntLit k))) ::
           concatStmtBlocks assigns,
         jIndex (jVar arr) (jIntLit idx))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Program assembly.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every generated member is a `public static Object f(){ .. }`, so this is the
-- only method shape the assembly needs.
\func constMethod (name : String) (body : Array JStmt) : JMember =>
  jMethod (\new JMethod true tObject name nil body)

\func compileDecl {t : JavaTarget} (kd : \Sigma Kername GlobalDecl) : JMember =>
  \let | name => mangleKername kd.1
  \in \case kd.2 \with {
    | constantDecl cb => \case cb.cstBody \with {
      | just body =>
        \let | o => compileExpr nil "" body
        \in constMethod name (appendStmts o.1 (jReturn o.2 :: nil))
      | nothing => \case lookupAxiom name javaAxioms \with {
        | just impl => constMethod name (jReturn impl :: nil)
        | nothing =>
          constMethod name
            (jThrow (jNew (tClass "RuntimeException")
                          (jStringLit ("axiom " ++ name ++ " not implemented") :: nil)) :: nil)
      }
    }
    | inductiveDecl _ =>
      jMemberComment ("inductive " ++ name ++ ": erased; values use Data(tag, fields)")
  }

\func compileDecls {t : JavaTarget} (ds : GlobalDeclarations) : Array JMember =>
  map (\lam d => compileDecl d) ds

-- `__main` holds the program's body; `main` is the entry point printing it.
--
-- The body is not evaluated by `main` itself but handed to `Rt.runMain` as a
-- one-argument closure (its argument is ignored, `Rt.BOX` is passed), which runs
-- and prints it on a thread with an explicitly requested stack size. Reason: λ□
-- `fix` compiles to plain, usually non-tail Java recursion, so evaluation depth
-- is bounded by the stack -- and the stack of the primordial thread running
-- `main` is set by the OS, not by the launcher's `-Xss`. Going through a thread
-- we create makes the bound a property of the generated program (tunable with
-- `-Dlambox.stack=<bytes>`) instead of a property of how it was launched; see
-- `Rt.runMain`.
\func compileMain {tgt : JavaTarget} (t : LBTerm) : Array JMember =>
  \let | o => compileExpr nil "" t
  \in constMethod "__main" (appendStmts o.1 (jReturn o.2 :: nil)) ::
      jMethod (\new JMethod true tVoid "main"
                 ((\new JParam (tArray (tClass "String")) "args") :: nil)
                 (jExprStmt (jCallStatic (rt ++ "runMain")
                              (jAnonFn rtFn "ignored"
                                 (jReturn (jCallStatic "__main" nil) :: nil) :: nil)) :: nil)) ::
      nil

-- Only the program-specific class is generated; `Fn`, `Data` (with its
-- human-readable `toString`) and `BOX` come from the hand-written runtime class
-- `runtime/Rt.java`, which must be compiled together with this output.
\func compileClass {t : JavaTarget} (p : LBProgram) : JClass =>
  \new JClass "Prog" (compileDecls p.1 ++A compileMain p.2)

-- The generator and the printer, composed. The int representation is explicit
-- here; `compileProgram` below fixes it to `targetLong`, the representation
-- closest to λ□'s int63 (see `JavaTarget`).
\func compileProgramWith (t : JavaTarget) (p : LBProgram) : String => printClass (compileClass p)

\func compileProgram (p : LBProgram) : String => compileProgramWith targetLong p

-- Print a generated program with `putStrLn` (Data.String -> console).
-- \import Debug
-- \func main => putStrLn (compileProgram (nil, prim (primInt, -44)))